#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055713} :activity/id #uuid "e7a68f82-01c3-5f8e-ac40-f72fcee7a2b7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049919 :topic/id #uuid "1e7769c8-1985-591a-bba8-e917166ea21d" :topic/math-academy-id 84} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054685 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054578 :question/id #uuid "5bb6861a-ea00-5644-bc16-d458cd2bb30f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7375"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae1738d1-3261-5769-ab80-0edcc76ab6f3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529091}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Y-Intercept of a Parabola Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "128181ff-b771-504f-87d8-8018de0e4105" :step/math-academy-id 27352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055952 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of the parabola ![](images/f2/f218c572691a74963cd3808bf4df2923122e84b65d1fabf1138bab6264b043b5.svg) shown below.\n\n![](images/45/450251899f728a3cc927b72fdb7655204828c8e0be0432cb08cee2462d6e5bfe.png)\n\nWe can describe many features of the graph using mathematical terminology:\n\n- The ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-intercepts of the parabola are at ![](images/81/8171bb8403bd612f56ef5f32f400f1aef8efeed51ea279d36779771fdea1ae0d.svg) and ![](images/77/779cec712506e0f798805963ab85841b46650fb22681d97a64aec4b85a47d07f.svg) We say that ![](images/b0/b064281df4bef2506521322c7477468b9cc565616e2c5b6f21000df6522399c1.svg) and ![](images/1f/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg) are the **roots** (or **zeros**) of the parabola.\n\n- The parabola intersects the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis at  ![](images/6a/6a0e2cbf9f56c3c989ad96f020fe8bb0f7c785ff6de8073c476232b95476ac13.svg). We say that ![](images/78/78d4465d85b55cee3832a9b598a2564b6a3fa9a072de8a1fb4b40ca884079670.svg) is the **![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept**. The ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept is the same as the constant term of the function.\n\n- The parabola has a \"turning point\" at  ![](images/ef/ef43561f9d825c9e79fb03dffafa744d386d0c2a5a07da903675bca120d59fd1.svg) which is called the **vertex**. In an upwards parabola (like the one shown above), the vertex is the minimum point, while in a downwards parabola, the vertex is the maximum point.\n\n- The vertical line  ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg) that passes through the vertex is called the **axis of symmetry**. The part of the parabola to the left of the axis of symmetry is the mirror image of the right, and vice versa.\n\n- This particular parabola is **upward opening** because it has a distinctive ![](images/13/13ab0b72e7963f3e74404b8b75f12654ece94c2c44e1fb09fdf230b976eb681b.svg) shape. A parabola ![](images/7c/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is upward opening if the quadratic coefficient is positive ![](images/d2/d2a0fc4d90b45e06999c89b1a40ff6f01116e9c429c195adbfced573f9e47660.svg)\n\n- Parabolas can also be **downward opening** with a distinctive ![](images/57/57872695daf3067460adec5a506cfb1457729de4eeac879eab9f7355570bac11.svg) shape. A parabola ![](images/7c/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is downward opening if the quadratic coefficient is negative ![](images/a1/a16d199f292dbd74941402b3d485cf107a7a985d28aeb13ab50ff6baf87f2d4b.svg)\n\nLet's take a look at an example of a downward opening parabola." :tutorial/id #uuid "38157cb0-baf6-5463-917a-3e22397a6104" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1643 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d642e4f2-9830-5f74-a1bf-20c4e26c8ac1" :step/math-academy-id 4546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054486 :question/id #uuid "d7d8ce77-98d8-54d6-9684-edde0ba9e1ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-1450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec5627a-4a37-5a14-9b39-1b43b422171f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529127}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "f933d693-32d6-57d7-a9d6-8e75f0fb5999" :step/math-academy-id 4547 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054487 :question/id #uuid "7b9c1bbd-6d33-5fb8-a207-02cf71ba8b0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-1451"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa081d4-f85a-5239-b034-b78894429611" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529178}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Parabola Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "4eea49a1-27f3-5ec1-8c5c-c52dee47ff51" :step/math-academy-id 4550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054722 :knowledge-point/c